#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078340 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/id #uuid "8012ecda-d2f4-52de-99de-7b2ebd635661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6676" :question/problem "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}.$ After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $T_S = 25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078341 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/id #uuid "e3e001a8-9294-59a4-82e6-b1122afdc72f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6677" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80^\\circ \\rm C,$ calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-0.25\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-\\int 0.25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-0.25t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0.$ \nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-0.25t+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-0.25t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80^\\circ \\rm C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}80 & =23+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 80 & =23+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44^\\circ\\textrm{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078374 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "9d7fbff5-7db5-53e4-a17e-3fb7ac7a0069" :question/math-academy-id "e-8957" :question/problem "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\nI. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, II is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12,$ and $a=50.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0731f6-6530-57d1-8101-d583dce27200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238171} {:answer/id #uuid "30cfb7a5-89a3-5976-92cb-41428186daaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238479} {:answer/id #uuid "afd882b4-452c-52f4-9d6a-2ad9a9e823b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2y(y-500),\\,y(0)=400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239582} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0874aa-3afc-56be-b514-dbaa87daff29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239832} {:answer/id #uuid "d2980861-6b7b-5001-a205-b1b4d310530b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30cfb7a5-89a3-5976-92cb-41428186daaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238479} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b7af48-1a47-5f7e-aa94-0ab3a1a923bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fb765b0-7266-558a-8445-d5e7e8dcfb07" :question/math-academy-id "q-105890" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=2,a=500,$ and ${y}_{0}=600.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=400<a=500.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc34311-544b-58ac-b0d4-5ffec4e304fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=80(y+20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238574} {:answer/id #uuid "4e20476d-f9bb-5130-8f9e-628e39957f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80y(20-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238728} {:answer/id #uuid "565d5a3d-b07c-5417-aca5-0f14a0b69ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(20-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238800} {:answer/id #uuid "90d5ff36-f97d-5e89-b7ec-2b83f4fa6dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=80y(y+20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239310} {:answer/id #uuid "e37d7ad7-c270-5174-be15-143df61c1f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e37d7ad7-c270-5174-be15-143df61c1f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240055} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2372051-f6b8-5f84-9685-5fee700182de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0fe545c-1008-5ec6-a573-a706bc6b026b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105898" :question/problem "Given that $y>20$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=80,$ and $a=20.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>20$ for all $t>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72fd79b0-8552-59fe-b722-7e484092a69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445938} {:answer/id #uuid "a014bd72-72d0-51e6-aca9-a65a27712eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-kT(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445940} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9d954e-d7a7-5d73-9350-3b59b8993edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445941} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c15d21-0615-53b3-b41f-f6213357630d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T+25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445946} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ceeca0-5ae8-54f6-8a11-ae6c66b94346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72fd79b0-8552-59fe-b722-7e484092a69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445938} :answer-field/id #uuid "385b574a-86cb-5cbc-a02b-25339e6092c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "246dbe47-9137-5b96-af87-04ddded3bed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105917" :question/problem "The rate at which a liquid cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-25).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37f8e72f-d006-593d-97e3-d9ede565e54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=60{y}^{2}-960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445930} {:answer/id #uuid "3e383006-56b2-5f3b-b5e0-6e135886c311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445931} {:answer/id #uuid "c269d59b-4c24-5a2e-8b68-d56b249ca9ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960+60y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445943} {:answer/id #uuid "c77bbc7e-9d55-561a-80a6-ebb8b2046097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445944} {:answer/id #uuid "fd079cb4-c93e-5568-a211-4982fc99b62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960y-60{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e383006-56b2-5f3b-b5e0-6e135886c311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445931} :answer-field/id #uuid "33d8e051-9b26-56c6-8fc8-5f8fb9709d58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daeefc4f-6d08-5a08-a1e7-5aed6435b59f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106652" :question/problem "Given that $y>16$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-60(y-16).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=60,$ and $a=16.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>16$ for all $t>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae1f843-84e5-5dc8-82c0-b0bc4b88a1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445929} {:answer/id #uuid "4815316b-c603-51a2-a732-458284f7c774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-