n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=60<a=90.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f2da53c-c7ca-5c98-8d85-a2aeb6fe3fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445927} {:answer/id #uuid "11ee4e76-6dd6-5534-86f3-54f86c2dcfb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445928} {:answer/id #uuid "7de9a5d8-fca6-54df-be6c-1bc3b035ed8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445939} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f10bfc-7725-5838-829c-638f4fde25b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30y(y-200),\\,y(0)=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445942} {:answer/id #uuid "cddd42ed-edf0-54e7-998a-1f3cc455ccb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=30y(y-200),\\,y(0)=210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11ee4e76-6dd6-5534-86f3-54f86c2dcfb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445928} :answer-field/id #uuid "85c16440-4f36-5799-80af-9e7a27fdee13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4a70316a-6e6e-5988-9abb-850863435cd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106658" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=30,a=200,$ and ${y}_{0}=210.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=100<a=200.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b80c3e-e59a-547b-a782-41612e4da31a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-67(T-145)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241227} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccf8a1b-29f1-5b0a-80ad-9958a78a6231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-145(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241422} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5e0f28-4de0-5f1e-ae81-d2cf901b0128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241889} {:answer/id #uuid "c6be0d3b-ed68-5a2f-af1a-64dc02f7559b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242933} {:answer/id #uuid "e362934b-110b-510b-8990-c6bccbecf578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-145)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6be0d3b-ed68-5a2f-af1a-64dc02f7559b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242933} :answer-field/id #uuid "93430b60-75e1-51bd-a136-498bba75fcbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ba4e3cf-6728-56eb-8731-1748c4ffae5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106883" :question/problem "A turkey reaches ${145}^{∘}\\text{F}$ in the oven. After removing the turkey from the oven, the turkey's temperature $T(t)$ decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the turkey? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42711072-4521-5fe7-9fb9-04bbe0e1aac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T+16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445932} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6c778e-b63c-5aa6-b1ce-5c050c5f7b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445934} {:answer/id #uuid "6056756e-9986-54df-b147-e00ce576b435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T+16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445936} {:answer/id #uuid "61ae82ec-9879-5f5d-ab92-a1997ae823f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T-16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445937} {:answer/id #uuid "f46e95ee-3654-530d-b86d-938504c88cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a6c778e-b63c-5aa6-b1ce-5c050c5f7b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445934} :answer-field/id #uuid "08edadb3-2531-58ae-aec8-c90d8179de19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "502009e8-d4ec-5acf-8f7e-4d70ee8a14f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-106932" :question/problem "The rate at which a cup of chocolate cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the chocolate in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${16}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={16}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-16).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8b5f534-9e32-5a0a-b2bc-2ba8e2c86a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242660} {:answer/id #uuid "b384362d-2277-54cd-9282-ce1d30347703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-90(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242745} {:answer/id #uuid "c6193ec8-264d-5ffa-8793-75573063af90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-90k(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242925} {:answer/id #uuid "d68d698b-8905-5477-8ba9-77a836e1555f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-19k(T-90)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243081} {:answer/id #uuid "fa680411-3657-5df5-b80b-ed27eaecce77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-90)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8b5f534-9e32-5a0a-b2bc-2ba8e2c86a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242660} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a02deb8-c057-587d-9e15-ea05b099a20b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f165134-d2dc-524e-9e90-e4a6ac3b7c02" :question/math-academy-id "q-106935" :question/problem "A container of water at a temperature of ${90}^{∘}\\text{C}$ is placed in a room. The temperature $T(t)$ of the water decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${19}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the water temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={19}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c073a7d-d446-5c47-a82c-ea083d458a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(T-30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241572} {:answer/id #uuid "88391a53-7653-5428-bd68-74e5bfdde4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT(T-30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242376} {:answer/id #uuid "a366829a-9610-524d-adb6-9faa478168dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(30-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242612} {:answer/id #uuid "a84300b7-b40c-55e6-8dd2-005494b5d423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T+30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242657} {:answer/id #uuid "aa620027-8710-5351-b4aa-bc6b10e6c92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa620027-8710-5351-b4aa-bc6b10e6c92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242670} :answer-field/id #uuid "7068eb8d-31e2-586e-b359-12d7347118f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "431988a0-8981-5d4d-9c5f-dd7ae32d4277" :question/math-academy-id "q-106936" :question/problem "The rate at which a pot of hot oil cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the oil? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40e0dff5-bfb1-5212-94c6-b1dadc022a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{96}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241760} {:answer/id #uuid "45e54fee-1ace-542a-8f41-af48ff15ba39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{92}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241808} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9fc5e0-ba4e-56c2-8bd8-2c6ee24e4cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242007} {:answer/id #uuid "a245084d-1bb7-5412-bc76-5d4162c98715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{76}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242601} {:answer/id #uuid "d36cea6e-e345-5856-9738-8be126828c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40e0dff5-bfb1-5212-94c6-b1dadc022a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{96}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241760} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa52215b-9a2a-5e79-b263-c1fe67ce6a74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b60de53-dea5-5dd2-908e-421b529445e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-107118" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cooling iron can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the iron is ${140}^{∘}C,$ calculate its temperature after $5$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cooling iron is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-0.1\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-\\int 0.1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-0.1t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-0.1t+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-0.1t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the iron was ${140}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =28+K \\\\ K & =112\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+112{e}^{-0.1t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the iron after $t=5$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(5)=28+112{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 5}≈{96}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123a42f8-2a35-5617-ba7d-f1b4d2c746b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241335} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc08283-0067-51f0-81a0-558fa4cdcf8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242008} {:answer/id #uuid "8346bdeb-1005-53c8-8841-f50949f617dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242327} {:answer/id #uuid "9c820d27-170e-5c1f-81bb-f4a2542fecd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242546} {:answer/id #uuid "ad515dcc-0231-5912-ac0e-df7d88012a7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8346bdeb-1005-53c8-8841-f50949f617dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242327} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bbd68ea-f66a-5917-98d1-898732b44fd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39da46ca-e83e-580d-8039-34194e24ef5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107122" :question/problem "A thermometer is taken out of a room where the air temperature is ${72}^{∘}F$  and brought outside where the temperature is ${15}^{∘}F,$ and its reading begins to decrease. After $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}F.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${25}^{∘}F?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred outside to ${15}^{∘}F.$*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={15}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-15| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-15>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-15 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-15 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-15 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =15+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${72}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=72,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =15+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 72 & =15+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=15+57{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=54,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54 & =15+57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{57} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57}) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${25}^{∘}F.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 15+57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ 57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =10 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{57} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${25}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57})}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})}≈9\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ae70af-887b-5770-9ba5-23db38cd4801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{31}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241332} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbae164-802b-58ab-91b2-3faf9544773d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241579} {:answer/id #uuid "4231eca7-f91a-5287-acdd-0be0665d65cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{43}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241773} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d27d97-2c83-55f0-970f-d85dcbf228a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242597} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3d7b5b-1863-5704-89a4-3e2c90453ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cbae164-802b-58ab-91b2-3faf9544773d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241579} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec7d5808-b789-5047-9d21-ccfd0fbce57b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ff863d3-8088-512b-858b-b0b33302607f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107129" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a compressor of a refrigerator can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the compressor is ${65}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the compressor after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the compressor of a refrigerator is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-0.12\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-\\int 0.12\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-21| & =-0.12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-21>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-21 & ={e}^{-0.12t+C} \\\\ T-21 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T-21 & =K{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T & =21+K{e}^{-0.12t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the compressor was ${65}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=65,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}65 & =21+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 65 & =21+K \\\\ K & =44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=21+44{e}^{-0.12t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the compressor after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=21+44{e}^{-0.12\\cdot 8}≈{38}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1deaf31a-b94f-5dbd-92e7-025ade20cd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272654} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7840ac-4dc2-5743-a317-909d0854aa2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273200} {:answer/id #uuid "80a9183c-594a-50ff-89ee-e5298cd3be92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273490} {:answer/id #uuid "87fc2c9b-f809-527a-9228-06e37aeb1131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273553} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fc440f-ad27-570f-b514-432bec694cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1deaf31a-b94f-5dbd-92e7-025ade20cd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272654} :answer-field/id #uuid "d15396bb-b0f6-5ba4-9f17-f68ca77b61da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b0ed3c5-b3aa-5668-ae2c-48b159839332" :question/math-academy-id "q-15384" :question/problem "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of ${50}^{∘}C.$ The thermometer is then transferred to a ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={10}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-10| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-10>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-10 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-10 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-10 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =10+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${50}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=50,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =10+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 50 & =10+K \\\\ K & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+40{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=40,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =10+40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 30 & =40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 10+40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =20 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈12\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1495237b-afbf-5205-a022-9f6acc5cbda9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{83}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272570} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8e38af-c5d0-507f-a1bd-aae08b27500d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273439} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f377d8-6bb6-5509-84d5-efce6120f034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273947} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd7cc63-77c2-5834-8ea5-22b666b6543c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274316} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb4ed67-f97a-54dc-81d8-37f939ad8898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbd7cc63-77c2-5834-8ea5-22b666b6543c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274316} :answer-field/id #uuid "217e660e-5140-5d1b-956d-f278238bb223" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "daa15db5-c1a1-535c-9031-79b2bb1e03e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15595" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cup of coffee can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the coffee is ${100}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the coffee after $6$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cup of coffee is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-0.05\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-\\int 0.05\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.05t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-0.05t+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-0.05t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the coffee was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the coffee after $t=6$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(6)=25+75{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 6}≈{81}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}]}
